{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Review remediation record (2026-08-21) — moved out of the ticket description to fit the 8000-char plan-review admission cap.\n\n## Review remediation (2026-08-21)\n- Removed the flagship \"new project-specific instance\": an absent-string subprocess assertion with no `returncode`/`check=True` already ships as the `fail-open oracle` antipattern at `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md:56-70`, landed by commit `6fb4d801c9` — the fix for `obvious-saline-gerbil` (`0e1d-c698-c38d-4c3e`), the very bug the dry run uses. Concern #12 now cites it rather than re-declaring it.\n- Removed the \"wall-clock budget standing in for an observable event\" as new: `code-review-tests.md:49` already flags a concurrency/race test whose PRIMARY synchronization is a timing sleep.\n- Reduced the do-not-flag list to citations: all seven entries were already false-positive guards (ii)-(x) at `code-review-tests.md:71-106` — Rule-5 structural greps (ii, `:82-88`), command-output greps with established liveness (iii, `:91-94`), exact-value asserts on pure functions (iv, `:94-96`), external-boundary mocks (vi, `:96`), bare \"no test for X\" (vii, `:97`), strict `xfail` with a named removal condition (viii, `:99-101`), and secondary settling sleeps (ix, `:102-103`). Restating them contradicted the plan's own \"single source of truth, not restated\" principle.\n- Kept only the three instances verified absent from the rubric: a fixture mutating state it did not create; an equivalence test whose two sides use different hardening helpers; an oracle encoding a policy preference the contract does not state.\n- Corrected the Scope path: `tests/unit/test_skill_frontmatter.py` does not exist. The real file is `tests/unit/test_check_skill_frontmatter.py`, and it needs no edit — it tests the script, not skill bodies.\n- Corrected the Testing claim: `scripts/check_skill_frontmatter.py` globs `**/SKILL.md` and validates only YAML frontmatter, so it never reads `phases/discovery.md`, `phases/verification.md`, or `references/patterns.md` — three of the four files in `file_impact`. Those edits are now proven by explicit greps in the acceptance criteria instead.\n- Added the missing consumer: `discovery.md:152` hardcodes \"which of the eleven concerns above\" in the output schema handed to every discovery subagent; adding #12 without updating it leaves the schema contradicting the concern list.\n- Added the missing scope item and its constraint: `SKILL.md` is in `file_impact` but had no criterion. Its frontmatter `description` enumerates the concerns and measures 980 characters against the 1024-character cap, leaving 44 characters of headroom.\n- Replaced ACs #1-#3, which were \"checked by reading the file\" and \"checked by the run transcript\" — no command, no oracle. Every criterion is now a command that fails on the current tree; the stochastic dry run is retained as an explicitly non-gating `[operator-attested]` item, carrying the provenance marker `project.measurement-provenance` triggers on.\n- Added the portable antipattern core in `references/patterns.md` (`project.portability` is blocking and active per `.rebar/criteria_routing.json`): rebar-janitor runs against non-rebar repos where `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md` does not exist, which would have left concern #12 with no antipattern list at all.\n- Dropped the hedged `docs/` page from Scope: `grep -rln \"janitor\" docs/` confirms no page documents the janitor concern list."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787370973717349001,"uuid":"a03880be-5835-4310-af06-65b926f0e782"}